notes[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-26T14:36:44.652Z","harness:claude-code","Root cause (macOS leg), reproduced in the official bash:3.2 image (3.2.57, same as macOS) and read in its lib/readline/histexpand.c and parse.y: history_expand first runs a quote tracker that, inside double quotes, skips the character after any backslash that precedes a double quote without pairing backslashes, so the rendered \\\\\" that ends a double-quoted argument with a trailing backslash (C:\\Program Files\\pm\\ in the fixture) leaves it inside double quotes; the later single-quoted '!bang!' then looks live and the expansion pass runs, and that pass breaks out of its loop after the first multibyte character (the U+2019 in smart’s), so the line is cut and bash reads the next physical line as the continuation of an open double quote: 12 arguments instead of 25. parse.y inhibits history expansion on a continuation line only inside a single-quoted string, so a double-quoted argument holding a line break is a second trigger (event not found, even without non-ASCII text); bash 5.3 handles both. Fix: quotePosixCommandArg single-quotes an argument holding !, CR or LF, or ending in a backslash; every other spelling is unchanged."
files[2]{path,scope,note}:
